<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles II, Duke of Parma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1799">1799</a>–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Charles Louis of Bourbon, Charles Louis, King of Etruria, Charles I, Duke of Lucca, Charles Louis of Bourbon-Parma, Louis II of Etruria · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon-parma">House of Bourbon-Parma</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles II, Duke of Parma, born Charles Louis of Bourbon-Parma, became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> as a small child in 1803 under his mother Maria Luisa's regency, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> ended the kingdom in 1807. He later ruled the Duchy of Lucca from 1824 to 1847 and then briefly Parma, an extravagant prince who spent much of his reign abroad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-parma.jpg" alt="Charles II, Duke of Parma" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_di_Carlo_Ludovico_di_Borbone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles Louis was born on 22 December 1799 at the Royal Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, the son of Louis, Prince of Piacenza, and Infanta Maria Luisa of Spain, and appears in Francisco de Goya's portrait of the family of Charles IV, in his mother's arms. After his father's death in 1803 he succeeded as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, aged three. In 1807 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> dissolved the kingdom and had him and his mother brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>A plan to give him a Kingdom of Northern Lusitania in northern Portugal came to nothing. His mother was arrested in 1811 and shut in a convent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, while the boy lived until 1815 in the household of his grandfather, the deposed Charles IV. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave Lucca to his mother, with him as heir, and promised him Parma on the death of the Empress Marie Louise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1820 he married Princess Maria Teresa of Savoy. They lived largely apart, and had two children, of whom only Charles III survived. On his mother's death on 13 March 1824 he became Duke of Lucca, but showed little interest in ruling. He travelled across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and left the government to his minister Ascanio Mansi, of whom it was said that while Charles Louis was the Duke, Mansi was king. In 1833 he returned and granted a general amnesty.</p><p>He studied biblical and liturgical texts, built a Greek Orthodox chapel at Marlia and was said to have flirted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>. Short of money, he sold the paintings of the Palatine Gallery in 1841. Under Austrian pressure he agreed in the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> of 1844 to cede Guastalla to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>. Riots in 1847 led him to abdicate Lucca on 4 October in favour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a>, for a pension.</p><p>When Marie Louise died on 17 December 1847 he took up Parma as Charles II. He was coldly received, and in the revolution of 1848 he granted a regency and then fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Austrian troops later occupied Parma and he took control of the government under their protection, but he soon abdicated for good in favour of his son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As Count of Villafranca he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. His son Charles III was assassinated on 27 March 1854, and his grandson Robert I was deposed in March 1860. Unlike other dethroned Italian monarchs, he welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> as a positive development. He died at Nice on 16 April 1883, aged 83, and was buried at Viareggio.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles II of Parma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bourbons then returned under <b>Charles II</b>, who fled in the revolution of 1848, when Piacenza voted to join Piedmont; Austrian troops restored the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Her son <b>Charles Louis</b>, Duke Charles I, ruled from 1824, an extravagant and unpredictable prince who spent much time abroad and left government to ministers.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-lucca">Duchy of Lucca</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 1803 his widow Maria Luisa of Spain governed as regent for their young son <b>Charles Louis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-etruria">Kingdom of Etruria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1853" aria-label="Madrid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles II, Duke of Parma"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4759 4110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4759 4110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1853">What was here in 1853? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
